WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.072 --> 00:00:03.198
- [David] This is a story of a plant,

00:00:03.198 --> 00:00:05.677
but not just any plant.

00:00:05.677 --> 00:00:07.198
It is the story of a plant

00:00:07.198 --> 00:00:10.845
that long, long ago once ruled the world.

00:00:10.845 --> 00:00:15.527
A plant that, today, is
the very last of its kind.

00:00:15.527 --> 00:00:18.042
(sad moan)

00:00:18.042 --> 00:00:21.297
It's this plant, behind
me, Encephalartos woodii.

00:00:21.297 --> 00:00:23.202
E. Woodii for short,

00:00:23.202 --> 00:00:26.981
and I've been looking
after it for over 20 years.

00:00:26.981 --> 00:00:30.109
It was named for British
botanist, John Medley Wood,

00:00:30.109 --> 00:00:33.811
who in 1895 discovered
it growing on a hillside

00:00:33.811 --> 00:00:35.294
on the coast of South Africa.

00:00:35.294 --> 00:00:38.098
A strange, handsome plant caught his eye,

00:00:38.098 --> 00:00:40.592
and he carefully removed
a small portion of it

00:00:40.592 --> 00:00:43.118
and had it shipped all the way to London

00:00:43.118 --> 00:00:44.530
to here, Kew Gardens,

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:48.284
where it's been for the last 117 years.

00:00:48.284 --> 00:00:52.117
But its history goes
much, much further back.

00:00:54.247 --> 00:00:59.096
You see, Encephalartos woodii,
is what is known as a cycad,

00:00:59.096 --> 00:01:02.782
and cycads have been around
for 300 million years.

00:01:02.782 --> 00:01:04.667
As the millennium rolled on,

00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:08.653
cycads flourished providing
shade for triceratops,

00:01:08.653 --> 00:01:10.843
a perch for pterodactyls,

00:01:10.843 --> 00:01:13.550
and a tasty snack for brontosauruses.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:15.646
At one point during the Jurassic,

00:01:15.646 --> 00:01:19.851
cycads made up 20% of
all the plants on Earth,

00:01:19.851 --> 00:01:23.234
and covered every corner of the globe.

00:01:23.234 --> 00:01:27.071
But the good times couldn't last forever.

00:01:27.071 --> 00:01:29.282
The dinosaurs went extinct.

00:01:29.282 --> 00:01:31.391
Ice ages came and went.

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:34.404
New modern plants, like
conifers and fruit trees,

00:01:34.404 --> 00:01:36.610
started pushing cycads out,

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:38.655
and the once proud population

00:01:38.655 --> 00:01:42.734
of E. Woodiis were reduced,
and reduced, and reduced

00:01:42.734 --> 00:01:45.866
until there was possibly only one left.

00:01:45.866 --> 00:01:50.738
One single solitary E. Woodii
growing quietly on a hillside.

00:01:50.738 --> 00:01:54.053
Which brings us right
back to John Medley Wood.

00:01:54.053 --> 00:01:56.533
At the time, he had no way of knowing

00:01:56.533 --> 00:01:59.253
just how rare his discovery was,

00:01:59.253 --> 00:02:02.139
but expedition after expedition

00:02:02.139 --> 00:02:06.354
in search of more E. Woodii
have proved fruitless.

00:02:06.354 --> 00:02:08.744
You see, cycads are dioecious,

00:02:08.744 --> 00:02:11.908
meaning, you need separate
male and female plants

00:02:11.908 --> 00:02:13.668
to create a new one,

00:02:13.668 --> 00:02:17.705
and this one happens to be a
male, a true lonely bachelor.

00:02:17.705 --> 00:02:20.309
If a female mate cannot be found

00:02:20.309 --> 00:02:23.981
it really will be the last of its kind.

00:02:23.981 --> 00:02:26.829
To this day, researchers
are still looking.

00:02:26.829 --> 00:02:30.996
After all, it's a big world,
there might just be a chance.

00:02:33.481 --> 00:02:36.977
In the meantime, it'll
have me to keep him company.

00:02:36.977 --> 00:02:39.977
(happy brass music)

